  "articleBody": "When planning aging-in-place remodeling, it's easy to make some common blunders that can undermine your goals for long-term safety and comfort. One big mistake is treating it just like any other cosmetic remodel. While aesthetics are nice, the real purpose is enhancing safety and accessibility, so don't overlook functional improvements. Another common pitfall is failing to address circulation paths, thresholds, or proper lighting, all of which are crucial for preventing falls. Skipping future needs, like potential walker or wheelchair access, is another one — you want changes that grow with you. Also, be sure not to overlook critical areas like the bathroom, which often presents the most significant risks. To avoid these, start with a thorough safety assessment. This helps identify and prioritize what really needs attention. And seriously, don't try to DIY everything; hiring a professional with aging-in-place experience is super important for structural work or complex accessibility designs, ensuring everything is safe, durable, and truly tailored to your needs.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}